  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 AM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nSome residual energy from Hurricane Simon over in the Pacific will \nlook to ride around the periphery of the ridge today with South \nTexas on the right flank of the area of lower pressure. Thanks to \nonshore flow ahead of this we have already started to see deep \nmoisture pooling into the area (PWATs 2.0\"+ in the region. The \npresence of these to factors will likely lead to a low chance for \nunsettled weather in the region Monday. Forcing is relatively weak \ntherefore would expect for the activity to be brief if it can get \ngoing. Coverage will likely not be widespread as guidance is \nportraying activity being confined to the northern Coastal Plains \nand Victoria Crossroads. We have observed some showers in the Gulf \nwaters in BROs CWA already tracking this way. \n\nAfter today, we will back to expecting daily seabreeze convection \npaired with weak shortwaves passing through that will lead to low \nchances for activity through the week. Warm temperatures will remain \nin place until Thursday. Guidance (GFS,Canadian,ECMWF) has came into \nbetter agreement that a cold front is expected to swing through the \nregion at the conclusion of the week. The timing will appears to be \nlate Friday into early Saturday. The strength of the front still \nremains a bit in question. With the differences in the guidance \nremaining confidence unfortunately will remain low at this time, \nthough we continue to monitoring the trends. Things will become more \nclear when we are in range of the higher resolution models.  \n\nOtherwise, today we likely still see continued minor coastal \nflooding in the region. Will have to continue to monitor this \npossibility over the next couple of days as we should begin a \ndowntrend, though an extension will remain in play.",
